<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>. See the sibling Contact rule for the
        reason - description is capped at 255 characters and the comment must be inside the root.

        AN ACCOUNT'S RECORD TYPE IS THE SHARING BOUNDARY FOR ITSELF AND EVERY CONTACT UNDER IT.
        Changing it does not move one record, it moves the whole subtree across the team
        boundary, silently and with no per-Contact audit trail. There is no legitimate
        end-user workflow that does this: a misfiled Account is an admin correction, made
        deliberately, not a mid-workflow edit.

        ADMINS ARE OUT OF SCOPE BY DESIGN (spec section 5) - a user with Modify All Data is
        already outside this boundary everywhere else in the org, so the rule does not attempt
        to stop them; $Permission was originally not consulted. If an exemption is ever needed,
        add a custom permission and gate on $Permission - never widen the rule itself.

        EXEMPTION ADDED 2026-08-17 (bug fix, LeadConvertServiceTest). This rule and
        LeadConvertService.stampConvertedPartyRecordTypes (added 2026-08-10, IR segregation)
        deadlocked: standard Database.convertLead does not map record types onto the Account it
        creates, so conversion follow-up UPDATEs the converted Account to Broker_Firm - a
        deliberate, one-time, post-creation RecordTypeId change that this rule cannot
        distinguish from the unrestricted reparenting it exists to block, because both are
        ISCHANGED(RecordTypeId) on an existing row. Database.update(..., false) swallowed the
        resulting failure silently, so RecordTypeId stayed null and
        conversionStampsBrokerRecordTypesOnContactAndAccount failed on "Actual: null". This was
        never a permission-set provisioning gap - see the corrected diagnosis in
        LeadConvertService.cls (stampConvertedPartyRecordTypes's header) for the full mechanism.

        Per this comment's own prescription above, the fix is a custom permission, not a
        widened rule: Lead_Convert_Account_RecordType_Stamp
        (force-app/main/default/customPermissions/), gated with
        NOT($Permission.Lead_Convert_Account_RecordType_Stamp) below. It is granted ONLY to
        Lead_Stage_Actions_Access (the real conversion-triggering population - it already gates
        who may convert a Lead at all) and DPEG_Admin_Access (the principal Apex tests and ad
        hoc admin conversions run as, measured live against usman-dpeg 2026-08-17 to hold
        DPEG_Admin_Access but not Lead_Stage_Actions_Access). See the custom permission's own
        in-file comment for the full call-path trace and the accepted tradeoff (a $Permission
        gate scopes by WHO the user is, not by WHICH CODE runs, so either grantee could in
        principle also hand-edit RecordTypeId while holding it - the same tradeoff this
        comment's original text already anticipated).
    -->
    <fullName>Record_Type_Is_Immutable</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>An account's record type cannot be changed - it is the sharing boundary for the account and every contact under it. See the XML comment in this file.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    NOT(ISNEW()),
    ISCHANGED(RecordTypeId),
    NOT($Permission.Lead_Convert_Account_RecordType_Stamp)
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>An account's record type cannot be changed. It controls which team can see this account and all of its contacts. Contact a system administrator if this account is filed under the wrong team.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
